### What does this question ask?

Generalize the second-highest salary pattern to any N. This tests parameterization and edge case handling.

### How do you find the Nth-highest salary?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class NthHighestSalary {
    public static Optional<BigDecimal> findNthHighest(
            List<Employee> employees, int n) {
        if (n < 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "N must be >= 1, got: " + n);

        return employees.stream()
            .map(Employee::getSalary)
            .distinct()
            .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
            .skip(n - 1)
            .findFirst();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        for (int n = 1; n <= 5; n++) {
            Optional<BigDecimal> salary = findNthHighest(employees, n);
            System.out.printf("Position %d: %s%n", n,
                salary.map(s -> "Rs." + s).orElse("N/A"));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Position 1: Rs.1800000
Position 2: Rs.1500000
Position 3: Rs.1400000
Position 4: Rs.1300000
Position 5: Rs.1200000
```

### How do you find the Nth-lowest?

```java
Optional<BigDecimal> nthLowest = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getSalary)
    .distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.naturalOrder())  // ascending
    .skip(n - 1)
    .findFirst();
```

### What about edge cases?

```java
// N exceeds distinct salaries: returns empty
Optional<BigDecimal> empty = findNthHighest(employees, 100);
System.out.println(empty.isPresent());  // false

// N = 1: returns the highest salary
Optional<BigDecimal> highest = findNthHighest(employees, 1);

// Single employee: only N=1 returns a value
List<Employee> single = List.of(new Employee("E001", "Rajesh",
    "TRADING", new BigDecimal("1800000")));
Optional<BigDecimal> pos2 = findNthHighest(single, 2);  // empty
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using skip(n) instead of skip(n-1).

```java
// WRONG: skip(n) skips n elements, giving position n+1
Optional<BigDecimal> wrong = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getSalary).distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(3)      // skips positions 1, 2, 3
    .findFirst(); // returns position 4, not 3!

// CORRECT: skip(n-1) for position n
Optional<BigDecimal> correct = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getSalary).distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(2)      // skips positions 1, 2
    .findFirst(); // returns position 3
```

### What about the time and space complexity?

| Operation | Complexity |
|---|---|
| map(getSalary) | O(n) |
| distinct() | O(n) with HashSet |
| sorted() | O(d log d) where d = distinct salaries |
| skip + findFirst | O(1) |
| Total | O(n + d log d) |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you return the employees at that salary?" Find the Nth salary first, then filter all employees matching it.
- "What is the time complexity?" O(n log n) for the sort.
- "How do you find Nth-highest per department?" Group first, then apply the pattern within each group.
